<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Mignano</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1139</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Mignano was concluded on 25 July 1139 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II of Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-ii">Pope Innocent II</a>, who had been captured by Roger's forces at Galluccio three days earlier. The pope recognised Roger as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> and Prince of Capua, legitimising the royal title first granted by the antipope Anacletus II in 1130. It secured the Norman kingdom's place among the states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p></div>
